from patch_lib import Doc
d = Doc("CORE-PR-HANDOFF-v12.md")
d.after_line("- **One handler, three sites, plus the run start", """- **A list of detectors must not hide the refusal (security major F1).** `combine` (`guard/injection.py:81`) returns `AnyOf` for more than one detector, and `AnyOf.check` calls `injection_check` on each entry with the default `flag` (`guard/injection.py:69-70`), so with `spend="raise"` set only on the outer call, a refusal inside the list became a flag and the run ended as an injection stop with the wrong reason that K6 counts as caught. v11's test passed with one detector and would fail with a list. Fix at the choke point: `guard/injection.py` holds one `ContextVar` `_SPEND_MODE` (default `"flag"`); `injection_check(detector, text, source, *, spend="flag")` sets it for the duration of the call (token reset in `finally`) and on `SpendRefused` either returns the flagged verdict `spend_refused` (`flag`) or re-raises (`raise`); `AnyOf.check` calls the inner `injection_check(d, text, source, spend=_SPEND_MODE.get())`, so the mode reaches every entry, and in `raise` mode the first refusal leaves the list (the other entries do not run). Callers that use `raise`: `Runtime._screen` and `k1b/loop.py:205`; `guard/judge.py:107` is read by the S-2a builder and gets `raise` if it runs inside a run or a replay (UNVERIFIED here). The approval summary and the shadow card keep `flag`. S-2a deletes the small S-1 handler in `Runtime.handle` (5.2b). Test (E0-S2-4): two detectors, one paid, window ended: the run ends `NEEDS_PERSON` with `limits.window_ended` and `caught_by` is `None`, and the same with one detector; mutant: `AnyOf` calls the inner check with the default.
- **Approval wording for a spend refusal (security minor F10).** A flagged verdict with `reason_code="spend_refused"` does not show the injection warning (`WARNING`, `approvals/requests.py:30`; picked at `approvals/requests.py:37`; the shadow card at `approvals/cards.py:79`): it shows its own sentence `WARNING_SPEND = "NOTE: the injection check could not run because a spend limit was reached. Read the inputs yourself before approving. "`. Test: the two sentences differ and each appears only for its own reason.""")
d.rep("it opens that scope around each `check`.", "it opens that scope around each `check` with `token = _SCOPE.set(scope)` and, in `finally`, `_SCOPE.reset(token)`, so the scope that was active before comes back (security minor F10: v11 did not say it restored the previous scope). The members of the combined detector are shared objects and take their scope from the context, never from the instance, so the same judge object used in a run and on the summary screen books each call against its own scope. Tests: a nested scope (run scope, wrapper inside it, back: the run scope is restored) and one judge instance used from both places.")
